\description{
The noise processes of DiffEqNoiseProcess.jl, for the \code{noise} argument of
\code{\link[=SDEProblem]{SDEProblem()}} and \code{RODEProblem()}, or on their own with
\code{NoiseProblem()}. A process is a function: \code{W(t)} returns its value at
\code{t}, bridged between saved points (the bridged point is then saved) or
simulated past the last one; \code{W$t} and \code{W$W} hold the saved path.
}
\details{
\code{WienerProcess(t0, W0, Z0)} is Brownian motion started at \code{W0} (a vector
gives independent components); \code{Z0} adds a second, independent process
(\code{W$Z}), used by solvers that need two Wiener processes. \code{RealWienerProcess()},
\code{SimpleWienerProcess()} (no rejection memory: adaptive rejections are an
error) and \code{CorrelatedWienerProcess(Gamma, t0, W0)} (covariance \code{Gamma} per
unit time) are variants. \code{GeometricBrownianMotionProcess(mu, sigma, t0, W0)} and \code{OrnsteinUhlenbeckProcess(Theta, mu, sigma, t0, W0)} are exact
samples of those processes; \code{CompoundPoissonProcess(rate, t0, W0)} counts
Poisson events with intensity \code{rate(u, p, t)} (with \code{computerates = FALSE}
the rate is fixed at \code{rate}). \code{BrownianBridge(t0, tend, W0, Wend)},
\code{GeometricBrownianBridge()}, \code{OrnsteinUhlenbeckBridge()} and
\code{CompoundPoissonBridge()} are the same processes conditioned to reach
\code{Wend} at \code{tend}.

\code{NoiseProcess(t0, W0, Z0, dist, bridge)} builds a process from its
increment distribution \code{dist(dW, W, dt, u, p, t, rng)} and its bridge
\code{bridge(dW, W, W0, Wh, q, h, u, p, t, rng)} (both return increments; \code{rng}
is \code{NULL} as R uses its own generator). \code{rswm = RSWM()} chooses the
rejection sampling with memory (\code{adaptivealg = "RSwM3"} keeps the rejected
future increments, \code{"RSwM0"} discards them, for state dependent noise).
\code{SimpleNoiseProcess()} has no rejection memory.

\code{NoiseGrid(t, W, Z)} is a fixed path interpolated linearly;
\code{NoiseFunction(t0, W, Z)} a path given by \code{W(u, p, t)};
\code{NoiseWrapper(source)} replays a saved process (a noise process, the
Brownian path \code{sol$W} of an SDE solution, or the solution itself), bridged
between its points, optionally reversed in time or restricted to the
components \code{indx}; \code{NoiseApproximation(source1, source2)} takes the path
from the solution of a differential equation integrator (see \code{\link[=init]{init()}});
\code{NoiseTransport(t0, W, RV, rv)} is \code{W(u, p, t, rv)} with a random variable
\code{rv} drawn from \code{RV()} at every reset; \code{VirtualBrownianTree(t0, W0, tend = , Wend = )} is Brownian motion evaluated by bisection from stored seeds, so
any value can be recomputed without storing the path; \code{BoxWedgeTail(t0, W0)} is two dimensional Brownian motion whose steps also sample the Levy
area \code{W$A} from its exact joint density with the increment (Gaines and
Lyons). \code{pCN(source, rho)} is the preconditioned Crank-Nicolson proposal
\verb{rho W + sqrt(1 - rho^2) W'} with an independent Brownian path \verb{W'}.

\code{accept_step()}, \code{reject_step()}, \code{calculate_step()}, \code{setup_next_step()}
and \code{save_noise()} step a process by hand, as an SDE integrator does:
\code{calculate_step(W, dt)} draws the pending increment \code{W$dW} over \code{dt},
\code{accept_step(W, dt)} adds it and draws the next one, \code{reject_step(W, dtnew)} replaces it by a bridged increment over \code{dtnew}, keeping the rest
for later steps. \code{resize_noise()}, \code{addat_noise()} and \code{deleteat_noise()}
change the number of components (as callbacks that resize the state do).
}
